/-!
# The singular locus of a morphism with equidimensional fibres

Let `f : X ⟶ S` be flat and locally of finite presentation, and suppose every nonempty fibre of
`f` is equidimensional of dimension `d`.  Then the closed subscheme of `X` cut out by the `d`-th
Fitting ideal of `Ω_{X/S}` has as its underlying set exactly the points at which `f` fails to be
smooth.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.mem_fittingIdeal_relativeDifferentials_iff_not_smoothAt`: a point of `X`
  lies in the `d`-th Fitting closed subscheme of `Ω_{X/S}` if and only if `f` is not smooth
  there.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.range_subschemeι_fittingIdeal_relativeDifferentials`: the same statement as
  an equality of subsets of `X`, identifying that closed subscheme with the nonsmooth locus.

## Implementation notes

The fibrewise dimension hypothesis is imposed only on the nonempty fibres, and it is stated as
the conjunction of `TopologicalSpace.Equidimensional` with the value `d` of the topological
Krull dimension.  Equidimensionality of the fibre through a point transfers the global dimension
of that fibre to the local dimension at the point, which is the quantity the numerical criterion
for smoothness of Tag 01V9 compares the number of generators of the relative cotangent space
against.

The Fitting side runs through the open complement `AlgebraicGeometry.fittingGeneratorOpen` of the
Fitting closed subscheme, whose points are those at which `Ω_{X/S}` is generated by `d` sections,
tested against the canonical residue field point of `x`.  Over the spectrum of a field a
quasi-coherent module of finite type is a finite-dimensional vector space, generation by `d`
sections there is the rank bound `d`, and base change identifies that vector space with the
relative cotangent space at `x`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 01V9](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01V9)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 05P8](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05P8)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0C3F](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0C3F)
-/

@[expose] public section

